Work-in-progress version 0.3 (build 20261121) of esxMON memory monitor and z80
disassembler for zx spectrum with file access supporting esxDOS 0.8.9 or
better(?). esxMON has nice 42x32 character display mode with single (bottom)
line for user input.  esxMON support simple line editing. you can delete
characters, and move left or right in the input line. editing is always in
'insert' mode. the input line is maximum 42 characters including the cursor.

One of the design(?) principles is to keep the code rommable i.e. no smc, and
no variables outside stack or ix indexed 256 bytes "sysvar" area. however, the
'r'egisters command somewhat broke this as some register values are accessed
directly to ram, not via ix.. bummer..

testing has been light at best so for bug reports email to:
  jouni.korhonen at iki.fi

Numbers are by default in hexdecimal except in directory listings. if they are
preceded with _ in the input or output line they are treated as decimal.
Memory 'p'oke command also understands ASCII strings. a string is opened with '
character and string ends to next white space of end of line.

Each command that takes optional <addr> as a parameter (marked as [] in help)
also changes the internal memory pointer to <addr>. This implies the next 'm',
'mm', 'd' or 'dd' command without <addr> uses that previously defined <addr>.

Worth noting that 'r'egister command does not handle SP properly. When
'c'alling a subroutine the SP is loaded according the current SP the esxMON
uses itself. PC is completely ignored.

CAPS or Symbol shift act as a break key for directory listings. 

The .command syntax in BASIC is the following:
.esxMON [<file> <addr> [<off> [<len>]]


Supported commands:
  m [<addr>]                       : show 7 lines of 8 byte blocks of RAM
                                     starting from <addr>.
  mm [<addr>]                      : show 15 lines of 8 byte blocks of RAM
                                     starting from <addr>.
  p <addr> [<num>|'<str>] ..       : poke bytes(!) or strings separated by
                                     white spaces into RAM starting from
								     <addr>.
  b <bank>                         : set RAM page at $C000 to <bank>.
  ls [<dir>]                       : show content of <dir> or current dir.
  cd <dir>                         : change current working directory.
  l <file> <addr> [<off> [<len>]]  : load <file> into RAM <addr> optionally
                                     at offset <off> (default 0) and optinally
                                     max <len> (default entire file)
  s <file> <addr> <len>            : save <file> from RAM <addr> and <len>
                                     bytes.
  f <addr1> <addr2> <byte>         : fill memory between <addr1> and <addr2>
                                     with value <byte>.
  d [<addr>]                       : Disassemble 7 opcodes starting from
                                     <addr>.
  dd [<addr>]                      : Disassemble 15 opcodes starting from
                                     <addr>.
  c <addr>                         : CALL subroutine at <addr>. Register 
                                     visible with 'r' as passed to subroutine.
  r [<16-bit reg> <value>          : dump registers or set a register.
  x                                : exit to BASIC.
  h                                : print help page.

